WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:03.000
Model size is often described by parameter count.

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:09.190
Labels such as 3B, 7B, 8B, 14B, 32B, and 70B indicate billions of parameters,

00:00:09.190 --> 00:00:13.215
although architecture and implementation affect actual memory, quality, and speed.

00:00:13.215 --> 00:00:17.000
Smaller models usually load faster, need less memory, and generate more quickly.

00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:21.000
They can be excellent for classification, rewriting, extraction, short assistance,

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:25.000
narrow domain workflows, and high-volume automation when evaluated carefully.

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:29.975
Larger models may offer stronger reasoning, knowledge, instruction following,

00:00:29.975 --> 00:00:31.960
or difficult-task performance, but they require more memory and compute.

00:00:31.960 --> 00:00:35.025
They may also increase latency, power use, deployment cost,

00:00:35.025 --> 00:00:36.975
and the consequences of concurrency.

00:00:36.975 --> 00:00:38.000
Bigger is not always better.

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:42.550
A large model running mostly on a slow CPU or constantly near memory limits may be less

00:00:42.550 --> 00:00:46.340
useful than a smaller model fully accelerated with room for context and other

00:00:46.340 --> 00:00:46.975
applications.

00:00:46.975 --> 00:00:49.185
Model family matters alongside size.

00:00:49.185 --> 00:00:53.000
Training data, architecture, tokenizer, prompt format, specialization, licensing,

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:56.135
and alignment can make two similarly sized models behave very differently.

00:00:56.135 --> 00:00:59.165
Compare task results, not parameter labels alone.

00:00:59.165 --> 00:01:01.910
Architecture can also change compute and cache behavior.

00:01:01.910 --> 00:01:05.215
Two models with the same headline parameter count may need different memory,

00:01:05.215 --> 00:01:08.000
context overhead, prompt formats, or runtime support,

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:09.960
so size categories remain planning approximations.

00:01:09.960 --> 00:01:11.960
Plan for the whole workload.

00:01:11.960 --> 00:01:16.000
A model that fits for one short prompt may fail with long context, vision components,

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:17.910
multiple sessions, or large outputs.

00:01:17.910 --> 00:01:22.000
Keep memory headroom for cache, runtime, operating system, and recovery from bursts.
